Here is a JavaScript function that takes an array of 5 numbers as input, checks if any of the numbers is a valid prime number, and prints the prime numbers to the console:
```javascript
/**
 * Checks if any of the numbers in the input array are prime numbers.
 * @param {number[]} numbers - An array of 5 numbers.
 */
function checkPrimeNumbers(numbers) {
  // Check if input array has exactly 5 elements
  if (numbers.length !== 5) {
    throw new Error('Input array must have exactly 5 elements');
  }

  // Loop through each number in the input array
  numbers.forEach((number) => {
    // Check if the number is a prime number
    if (isPrime(number)) {
      // If it is, print the number to the console
      console.log(`${number} is a prime number`);
    }
  });
}

/**
 * Checks if a number is a prime number.
 * @param {number} number - The number to check.
 * @returns {boolean} True if the number is prime, false otherwise.
 */
function isPrime(number) {
  // Numbers less than 2 are not prime
  if (number < 2) {
    return false;
  }

  // Loop through all numbers up to the square root of the input number
  for (let i = 2, sqrt = Math.sqrt(number); i <= sqrt; i++) {
    // If the number is divisible by any of these numbers, it's not prime
    if (number % i === 0) {
      return false;
    }
  }

  // If we reach this point, the number is prime
  return true;
}

// Example usage:
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
checkPrimeNumbers(numbers);
```
In this code, we define two functions: `checkPrimeNumbers` and `isPrime`. The `checkPrimeNumbers` function takes an array of 5 numbers as input and loops through each number, calling the `isPrime` function to check if it's a prime number. If it is, it prints the number to the console.

The `isPrime` function checks if a single number is prime by looping through all numbers up to the square root of the input number and checking if the number is divisible by any of them. If it is, it's not a prime number.

We also include an example usage of the `checkPrimeNumbers` function with an array of 5 numbers.

Note that this code uses a simple trial division method to check for primality, which is sufficient for small numbers. For larger numbers, you may want to use a more efficient algorithm, such as the Miller-Rabin primality test.

