Here is a Java solution to the problem:

```java
public class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        // 3^19 is the largest power of three that is less than or equal to 2^31 - 1
        // any power of three larger than this would exceed the maximum limit
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }
        while (n % 3 == 0) {
            n /= 3;
        }
        return n == 1;
    }
}
```

This solution uses a while loop to divide the input number `n` by 3 as long as it is divisible by 3. If `n` is a power of three, then after the loop, `n` should be equal to 1.

However, the follow-up question asks to solve it without loops or recursion. Here is an alternative solution that uses the mathematical property of logarithms:

```java
public class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }
        double x = Math.log(n) / Math.log(3);
        return x == Math.floor(x);
    }
}
```

This solution uses the `Math.log` function to calculate the logarithm of `n` base 3. If `n` is a power of three, then the result should be an integer, which means that it is equal to its floor value.

Example use cases:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    Solution solution = new Solution();
    System.out.println(solution.isPowerOfThree(27)); // true
    System.out.println(solution.isPowerOfThree(0)); // false
    System.out.println(solution.isPowerOfThree(-1)); // false
}
```

