printPrimeNumbers(numbers);
```

First, the `isPrime` function checks if a number is prime. It returns `false` if the number is less than or equal to 1, as numbers less than 2 are not prime. Then, it checks if the number is divisible evenly by any number from 2 to the square root of the number. If it is, the function returns `false`, otherwise it returns `true`.

The `printPrimeNumbers` function checks if the input array has exactly 5 numbers. If not, it prints an error message to the console and returns. Then, it iterates over each number in the array and calls `isPrime` on each. If the number is prime, it prints it to the console.

You can test this code by calling `printPrimeNumbers` with an array of 5 numbers. In the example above, we've tested it with an array of the first 5 prime numbers.

Here's an example of how you can create a modal with a fake chat history using HTML and CSS:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Modal Chat History</title>
  <style>
    /* The Modal (background) */
    .modal {
      display: none; /* Hidden by default */
      position: fixed; /* Stay in place */
      z-index: 1; /* Sit on top */
      padding-top: 100px; /* Location of the box */
      left: 0;
      top: 0;
      width: 100%; /* Full width */
      height: 100%; /* Full height */
      overflow: auto; /* Enable scroll if needed */
      background-color: rgb(0,0,0); /* Fallback color */
      background-color: rgba(0,0,0,0.4); /* Black w/ opacity */
    }

    /* Modal Content */
    .modal-content {
      background-color: #fefefe;
      margin: auto;
      padding: 20px;
      border: 1px solid #888;
      width: 80%;
    }

    /* The Close Button */
    .close {
      color: #aaaaaa;
      float: right;
      font-size: 28px;
      font-weight: bold;
    }

    .close:hover,
    .close:focus {
      color: #000;
      text-decoration: none;
      cursor: pointer;
    }

    /* Style for chat messages */
    .chat-message {
      padding: 10px;
      border-radius: 5px;
      margin-bottom: 10px;
    }

    /* Style for bot messages */
    .bot-message {
      background-color: #007bff;
      color: white;
      float: left;
      clear: both;
    }

    /* Style for human messages */
    .human-message {
      background-color: #f4f4f4;
      color: black;
      float: right;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<!-- Trigger/Open The Modal -->
<button id="myBtn">Open Modal</button>

<!-- The Modal -->
<div id="myModal" class="modal">

  <!-- Modal content -->
  <div class="modal-content">
    <span class="close">&times;</span>
    <!-- Fake chat history -->
    <div class="chat-message bot-message">
      Hello there!
    </div>
    <div class="chat-message human-message">
      Hi, how can you help me today?
    </div>
    <div class="chat-message bot-message">
      I'm here to answer any questions you have!
    </div>
    <div class="chat-message human-message">
      That's great, thanks!
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

<script>
// Get the modal
var modal = document.getElementById("myModal");

// Get the button that opens the modal
var btn = document.getElementById("myBtn");

// Get the <span> element that closes the modal
var span = document.getElementsByClassName("close")[0];

// When the user clicks the button, open the modal
btn.onclick = function() {
  modal.style.display = "block";
}

// When the user clicks on <span> (x), close the modal
span.onclick = function() {
  modal.style.display = "none";
}

// When the user clicks anywhere outside of the modal, close it
window.onclick = function(event) {
  if (event.target == modal) {
    modal.style.display = "none";
  }
}
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

In this example, the modal is initially hidden and can be opened by clicking the "Open Modal" button. The modal contains the fake chat history with two messages from the human and two messages from the AI chatbot. The messages are styled using CSS classes.

In TypeScript, you can use type assertions to tell the type system that a value has a specific type, even if the type system cannot determine it on its own. Type assertions are similar to type casts in other languages, and they allow you to assert that a value is of a specific type.
Here's an example of how you can use type assertions to assert that a value has a specific type:
```typescript
let myValue: number | string = "hello";
let myNumber: number = (myValue as number) * 2;
```
In this example, `myValue` is declared as a `number | string`, meaning it could be either a number or a string. However, we know that in practice, it will always be a string. We can use a type assertion to tell TypeScript to treat `myValue` as a string instead of a `number | string`. This allows us to perform operations that are specific to strings without TypeScript complaining about possible type mismatches.
Here's how you could use a type assertion in this case:
```typescript
let myValue: number | string = "hello";
let myLength: number = (myValue as string).length;
```
In this example, we assert that `myValue` is a string using `(myValue as string)`, which allows us to access the `length` property of the string. Without the type assertion, TypeScript would not know that `myValue` is a string and would not allow us to access the `length` property.
TypeScript also supports a newer syntax for type assertions, which uses the `<>` syntax:
```typescript
let myValue: number | string = "hello";
let myLength: number = (<string>myValue).length;
```
Both syntaxes are equivalent and can be used interchangeably. However, the newer syntax is more concise and is generally preferred over the older syntax.

