```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
#include <cassert>

// This program reads a text file and counts the occurrences of a specific word.
// Usage: count_word_occurrences("example.txt", "word");

int count_word_occurrences(const std::string& filename, const std::string& word) {
    std::ifstream file(filename);
    assert(file.is_open() && "File could not be opened");

    std::string line;
    int count = 0;
    while (getline(file, line)) {
        std::string::size_type pos = 0;
        while ((pos = line.find(word, pos)) != std::string::npos) {
            if ((pos == 0 || !std::isalpha(line[pos - 1])) &&
                (pos + word.length() == line.length() || !std::isalpha(line[pos + word.length()]))) {
                count++;
            }
            pos += word.length();
        }
    }
    file.close();
    return count;
}

// Function to check if the word counting works correctly
void check_function() {
    int occurrences = count_word_occurrences("example.txt", "word");
    assert(occurrences == 5 && "The word count is incorrect");
    std::cout << "Test passed: The word 'word' appears " << occurrences << " times." << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    // Call the check function to verify the correctness of the word counting
    check_function();
    return 0;
}
```

In this code snippet, we have a function `count_word_occurrences` that takes a filename and a word as arguments and returns the number of times the word appears in the file. The function reads the file line by line and searches for the word in each line. It uses a simple algorithm to ensure that the word is not a substring of another word (for example, "word" in "words").

The `check_function` is used to verify that the `count_word_occurrences` function works correctly. It assumes that the file "example.txt" exists and contains the word "word" exactly 5 times. If the word count is correct, the function prints a success message; otherwise, it asserts and prints an error message.
